INSTALL
=======

Requirements
------------
- An NVIDIA GPU with a working CUDA driver
- CUDA Toolkit (nvcc), version 10.0 or newer (tested against a Coursera lab
  container with CUDA's runtime API + C++17 support)
- A C++17-capable host compiler (installed alongside the CUDA Toolkit)
- GNU Make

No other libraries are required: image I/O is a small hand-written PPM
reader/writer (src/ppm_io.*), and image processing uses two hand-written
CUDA kernels (src/kernels.cu) -- there is nothing extra to install.

Building
--------
From the project root:

    make build

This compiles src/main.cpp, src/ppm_io.cpp, and src/kernels.cu with nvcc
and produces bin/cuda_batch_filter.

If nvcc reports it cannot find cuda_runtime.h or -lcudart, your CUDA
Toolkit include/lib paths differ from the defaults assumed in the
Makefile (/usr/local/cuda/include, /usr/local/cuda/lib64). Edit the
INCLUDES / LIBDIRS variables at the top of the Makefile to match your
installation (`which nvcc` and `nvcc --version` are a good starting
point for locating it).

Running
-------
    ./run.sh [input_dir] [output_dir] [blur_radius]

Defaults: input_dir=data/input, output_dir=data/output, blur_radius=2.
This also writes a timestamped proof_of_execution_*.log file in the
project root.

Or invoke the executable directly:

    ./bin/cuda_batch_filter data/input data/output 2

Cleaning
--------
    make clean

Removes the built executable and any generated data/output contents.
